      },
      "text": "The four bonding types produce drastically different mineral properties:"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md#006",
      "path": [
        "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry, git-history:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:mineral-chemistry",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineral Chemistry and Bonding",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nBONDING TYPES AND THEIR MINERAL CONSEQUENCES\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                  |\n|  IONIC BONDING                                                   |\n|  Electrons fully transferred between atoms                       |\n|  Electrostatic attraction between +/- ions                       |\n|  → Moderately hard, brittle, good cleavage, dissolves in water   |\n|  Examples: Halite (NaCl), fluorite (CaF₂), calcite (CaCO₃)       |\n|                                                                  |\n|  COVALENT BONDING                                                |\n|  Electrons shared between atoms                                  |\n|  → Very hard, poor cleavage, insoluble                           |\n|  Examples: Diamond (C-C in 3D), quartz (Si-O), corundum (Al-O)   |\n|  Note: Diamond is the hardest mineral → purely covalent 3D net   |\n|                                                                  |\n|  METALLIC BONDING                                                |\n|  Delocalized electron sea                                        |\n|  → Soft to moderate, ductile, malleable, opaque, shiny           |\n|  Examples: Native gold, copper, silver, platinum                 |\n|                                                                  |\n|  VAN DER WAALS (molecular) BONDING                               |\n|  Weak intermolecular forces between layers                       |\n|  → Very soft, perfect basal cleavage, greasy feel                |\n|  Examples: Graphite (C layers), talc (Mg silicate sheets)        |\n|                                                                  |\n|  MIXED BONDING (most minerals)                                   |\n|  Silicates: covalent Si-O + ionic Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, etc.               |\n|  Sulfides: largely covalent with metallic character              |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
