      "text": "## Pauling's Rules — Why Mineral Structures Work"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md#009",
      "path": [
        "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry, git-history:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:mineral-chemistry",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineral Chemistry and Bonding",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Linus Pauling (1929) derived five rules from electrostatics that predict stable ionic crystal structures. These govern most oxide, carbonate, phosphate, and silicate minerals."
    },
    {
      "id": "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md#010",
      "path": [
        "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry, git-history:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:mineral-chemistry",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineral Chemistry and Bonding",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Rule 1: Radius Ratio Principle**"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md#011",
      "path": [
        "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry, git-history:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:mineral-chemistry",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineral Chemistry and Bonding",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The coordination number (how many anions surround a cation) depends on the ratio of ionic radii:"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md#012",
      "path": [
        "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry, git-history:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:mineral-chemistry",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineral Chemistry and Bonding",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nCATION/ANION RADIUS RATIO → COORDINATION NUMBER\n+--------------------------------------------------+\n| r_c/r_a ratio  | Coord. # | Geometry             |\n+--------------------------------------------------+\n| 0.155–0.225    |    3     | Triangular (CO₃²⁻)   |\n| 0.225–0.414    |    4     | Tetrahedral (SiO₄)   |\n| 0.414–0.732    |    6     | Octahedral (MgO₆)    |\n| 0.732–1.000    |    8     | Cubic                |\n| ≥ 1.000        |   12     | Cuboctahedral        |\n+--------------------------------------------------+"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md#013",
      "path": [
        "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry, git-history:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:mineral-chemistry",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineral Chemistry and Bonding",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Si⁴⁺ in O²⁻: r_c/r_a ≈ 0.26 → tetrahedral coordination\n→ This is why SiO₄ tetrahedra are the universal silicate building block\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md#014",
      "path": [
        "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry, git-history:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:mineral-chemistry",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineral Chemistry and Bonding",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Rule 2: Electrostatic valence principle.** The charge on each anion is exactly balanced by the sum of electrostatic bond strengths from surrounding cations: s = z/CN. This determines which polyhedral linkages are locally neutral and therefore stable."
    },
    {
      "id": "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md#015",
      "path": [
        "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry, git-history:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:mineral-chemistry",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineral Chemistry and Bonding",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Rule 3: Polyhedral linking stability.** Shared edges destabilize a structure more than shared corners; shared faces destabilize it most. The reason: bringing cation centers closer together increases cation–cation repulsion. SiO₄ tetrahedra in silicates share only corners (never edges or faces) for exactly this reason."
    },
    {
      "id": "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md#016",
      "path": [
        "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry, git-history:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:mineral-chemistry",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineral Chemistry and Bonding",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Rule 4: Cation avoidance.** In crystals with multiple cation types, high-charge/small-radius cations avoid sharing polyhedral elements with each other. The Coulomb repulsion between Si⁴⁺ centers is so strong that SiO₄ tetrahedra never share edges."
    },
    {
      "id": "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md#017",
      "path": [
        "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry, git-history:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:mineral-chemistry",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineral Chemistry and Bonding",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Rule 5: Parsimony.** The number of structurally distinct environments in a crystal is minimized. Nature tends toward the fewest distinct site types consistent with the stoichiometry. This explains why most minerals have only one or two inequivalent cation sites despite complex chemistry."
    },
    {
      "id": "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md#018",
      "path": [
        "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
