      "text": "## Solid Solution Series"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md#021",
      "path": [
        "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry, git-history:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:mineral-chemistry",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineral Chemistry and Bonding",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Minerals can substitute chemically similar ions within the same crystal structure up to certain limits. The substitution is controlled by:\n- Similar ionic radius (within ~15%)\n- Same valence, OR charge-coupled substitution"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md#022",
      "path": [
        "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry, git-history:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:mineral-chemistry",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineral Chemistry and Bonding",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nKEY SOLID SOLUTION SERIES\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|  OLIVINE SERIES: (Mg,Fe)₂SiO₄                                    |\n|  Forsterite ————————————————————→ Fayalite                       |\n|  Mg₂SiO₄           continuous         Fe₂SiO₄                    |\n|  (pale green)                          (black)                   |\n|  Mg²⁺ and Fe²⁺ have nearly identical radii (0.72 vs 0.78 Å)      |\n|                                                                  |\n|  FELDSPAR SERIES: Two distinct series                            |\n|  Albite ————————————————————→ Anorthite   (Plagioclase)          |\n|  NaAlSi₃O₈                    CaAl₂Si₂O₈                         |\n|  Na⁺ + Si⁴⁺ ↔ Ca²⁺ + Al³⁺  (charge-coupled substitution)         |\n|                                                                  |\n|  GARNET GROUP: Many end-members                                  |\n|  Pyrope (Mg) ↔ Almandine (Fe) ↔ Spessartine (Mn)                 |\n|  Grossular (Ca-Al) ↔ Andradite (Ca-Fe) ↔ Uvarovite (Ca-Cr)       |\n|                                                                  |\n|  TOURMALINE: Most complex solid solution in common minerals      |\n|  X Y₃ Z₆ (BO₃)₃ Si₆O₁₈ (OH,F)₄  with many X,Y,Z substitutions    |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md#023",
      "path": [
        "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry, git-history:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry]",
