      "text": "## Polymorphism — Same Formula, Different Structure"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md#026",
      "path": [
        "01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry, git-history:mineralogy:01-mineral-chemistry]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/01-MINERAL-CHEMISTRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:mineral-chemistry",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[mineral, chemistry]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineral Chemistry and Bonding",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Same composition, different unit cell geometry — and the physical properties diverge completely. Polymorphism is the crystallographic version of the same fact that matters across materials science: macroscopic behavior is a function of structure, not just composition. The bonding connectivity and geometry determine the energy landscape, which determines hardness, cleavage, conductivity, and optical behavior."
    }
